  "explanation": {
    "summary": "の is a small glue word that joins two nouns. The noun before の is the owner or the describer, and the noun after の is the actual thing. So 私の コーヒー is literally 'I's coffee', meaning 'my coffee'. English uses 'my', 'your', ''s' or 'of', but Japanese uses the same pattern every time: [A] の [B]. The whole group 'A の B' then works like one big noun, so you can put it anywhere a single noun can go, such as before です or before は.",
    "structure_notes": [
      "Order: owner first, thing second. ゆきの ともだち = Yuki's friend (the friend belongs to Yuki). If you swap them, the meaning changes.",
      "The main noun always comes last. In みせの メニュー the thing is the menu, and みせ (restaurant) tells you which menu.",
      "Use 私の for 'my', and a name + の for 'your' or 'his/her': けんの ケーキ = Ken's cake. Japanese often uses the person's name where English says 'your'.",
      "You can chain の: 私の ともだちの みせ = my friend's restaurant. Read it left to right: I → friend → restaurant.",
      "A の B is one noun block, so it fits the slots you already know: これは [A の B] です。 / [A の B] は [Noun] です。 / これは [A の B] じゃないです。"
    ],
    "connects_to": "In 〜は〜です, 〜ですか and 〜じゃないです every slot held one noun. Now any of those nouns can grow into a bigger 'A の B' block, and the rest of the sentence stays exactly the same."
  },
